---
// Results page for the Local LLM Lab benchmark.
// All numbers come from summary.json, exported by Local_LLM_Lab/scripts/export_summary.py.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import summary from '../../data/llm-lab/summary.json';

const repoUrl = 'https://github.com/jackrz412/local_llm_lab';
const findingsUrl = `${repoUrl}/blob/main/results/Analyzed%20Results/FINDINGS.md`;

const models = summary.models;
const byName = Object.fromEntries(models.map((m) => [m.name, m]));
const fastest = byName['llama3.2:3b'];
const largest = byName['gemma3:12b'];

const totalAnswers = models.reduce((sum, m) => sum + m.pass_rates.all.strict.total, 0);
const timeRatio = Math.round(largest.seconds_per_correct / fastest.seconds_per_correct);
const totalFails = models.reduce(
  (sum, m) => sum + Object.values(m.failure_modes).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0),
  0,
);
const formatFails = models.reduce((sum, m) => sum + (m.failure_modes.format ?? 0), 0);
const gemmaComparable = largest.pass_rates.comparable.json_accepted;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Local LLM Lab | Jack Roberts"
  description="Three open LLMs benchmarked on a 16 GB Mac: accuracy, speed, and why answers failed."
>
  <p><a href="/">← Home</a></p>

  <!-- Headline + takeaway -->
  <header>
    <h1>Local LLM Lab</h1>
    <p>
      Three open models, run entirely on a 16 GB Mac. The biggest one was the most accurate,
      but it took {timeRatio}× as long per correct answer. Many of the "failures" were only
      about formatting.
    </p>
  </header>

  <!-- Stat row -->
  <section aria-label="Key numbers">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>{models.length}</strong> models</li>
      <li><strong>{totalAnswers}</strong> graded answers</li>
      <li><strong>{timeRatio}×</strong> time per correct answer, largest vs smallest</li>
      <li><strong>{formatFails} of {totalFails}</strong> fails were format-only</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- Model color key -->
  <section aria-label="Models">
    <ul>
      {models.map((m) => (
        <li>
          <span class="swatch" data-model={m.name}></span>
          <code>{m.name}</code> ({m.size_gb.toFixed(1)} GB)
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- Finding 1 -->
  <section id="accuracy-vs-time">
    <h2>1. More accurate, but much slower</h2>
    <p>
      <code>gemma3:12b</code> passed {largest.pass_rates.all.strict.passed} of
      the {largest.pass_rates.all.strict.total} answers and gave the same verdict on every run
      of every prompt. It needed {largest.seconds_per_correct} seconds per correct answer,
      against {fastest.seconds_per_correct} for <code>llama3.2:3b</code>.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <div id="chart-accuracy"></div>
      <figcaption>
        Each dot corresponds to one model. Higher is more accurate, and further left is faster per correct answer.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- Finding 2 -->
  <section id="scorecard">
    <h2>2. A quarter of the fails were only about format</h2>
    <p>
      Of the {totalFails} fails, {formatFails} were correct JSON wrapped in a Markdown code
      block. Accept that, and leave out the two prompts every model failed, and Gemma
      passes {gemmaComparable.passed} of {gemmaComparable.total}.
    </p>
    <label>
      <input type="checkbox" id="accept-json" />
      Accept JSON in code blocks
    </label>
    <div class="placeholder">
      Heatmap: passes out of {summary.runs_per_prompt} runs,
      for {summary.scorecard.prompts.length} prompts × {models.length} models. The toggle switches
      between the strict and code-block-accepted grids.
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Finding 3 -->
  <section id="failure-modes">
    <h2>3. Why answers failed</h2>
    <p>
      The smaller models' fails were real errors: wrong arithmetic, the wrong economic law,
      broken code and invented data. Even passing answers could contain made-up facts.
    </p>
    <div class="placeholder">
      Chart: failure-mode counts per model, as horizontal bars.
    </div>

    <h3>In their own words</h3>
    {summary.quotes.map((q) => (
      <figure>
        {q.excerpts.map((e) => (
          <blockquote>
            <p>Run {e.runs.join(' and ')}:</p>
            <pre>{e.text}</pre>
          </blockquote>
        ))}
        <figcaption>
          <code>{q.model}</code>, <code>{q.prompt_id}</code>: {q.caption}
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    ))}
  </section>

  <!-- Under the hood -->
  <section id="under-the-hood">
    <h2>Under the hood: speed follows size</h2>
    <p>
      To produce each token, a model reads almost all of its weights from memory, so speed is
      roughly memory bandwidth ÷ model size.
    </p>
    <div class="placeholder">
      Chart: tokens/s (y) vs model size in GB (x), one point per model, with the
      curve {summary.hardware.memory_bandwidth_gb_s} GB/s ÷ size.
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Method & limitations -->
  <details>
    <summary>Method &amp; limitations</summary>
    <h3>Method</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Models ran locally through Ollama on an {summary.hardware.machine}, with default
        settings. Each answered 12 prompts {summary.runs_per_prompt} times.
      </li>
      <li>
        Two prompt sets: general tasks (factual recall, arithmetic, summary, JSON output, a
        trap question) and Okun's Law tasks. The Okun's Law tasks are graded against a
        regression on public FRED data, 1960–2024 (slope {summary.okun_ground_truth.slope}).
      </li>
      <li>
        Answers were graded by automatic checks, with 9 graded by hand. Each fail was given one
        failure mode: its main cause.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Limitations</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Small sample: 12 prompts × 3 runs. A difference of a few fails is not meaningful.</li>
      <li>One machine and default settings, so timings vary between runs.</li>
      <li>Automatic checks rely on keywords and patterns, and one person did the hand grading.</li>
      <li>
        The <code>okun-interpret</code> prompt was flawed, and <code>okun-compute</code> was
        designed to fail. That's why the comparable rates leave out both.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <!-- Links -->
  <section id="links">
    <h2>Code and data</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={repoUrl}>Source code on GitHub</a></li>
      <li><a href={findingsUrl}>Full write-up (FINDINGS.md)</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import * as Plot from '@observablehq/plot';
  import summary from '../../data/llm-lab/summary.json';

  // 1. Share the data
  const data = summary.models.map((m) => ({
    model: m.name, 
    passRate: m.pass_rates.all.strict.passed / m.pass_rates.all.strict.total, 
    secondsPerCorrect: m.seconds_per_correct, 
  }));

  //2. Describing the chart
  const chart = Plot.plot({
    marks: [
      Plot.dot(data, { x: 'secondsPerCorrect', y: 'passRate' }),
    ],
  });

  // 3. Putting everything on the page
  document.querySelector('#chart-accuracy').append(chart);
</script>

<style>
  /* Temporary: make the chart placeholders visible until the real charts are built. */
  .placeholder {
    border: 2px dashed gray;
    padding: 2rem 1rem;
  }
</style>
